<BaseLayout
  page="code-class-book.html"
  title="A Book by Script"
  description="The tooling behind the Design VI class book on Malé, Villimalé and Hulhumalé: page templates drawn by Python, InDesign driven by script, and the finished book turned into a website in about a day."
  shareDescription="A 198-page class book by 31 students, set with scripts, and its web edition built in about a day."
  image="assets/projects/class-book/site-home.webp"
  fonts={["plex-mono"]}
  bodyClass="project-page page-ink pk"
>
    <Navbar page="code-class-book.html" active="coding" />

    <!--
      A Coding entry page (docs/design-personal.md, section 05) for the ARC307 class book,
      October 2026. Facts come from the Book folder (README, CLAUDE.md, HANDOVER.md, the
      scripts, file dates) and the public repository seven-leven/arc307-book. No student
      names: the images used are the cover, the style sheet, the contents, the web design
      board and two screenshots of the public site.
    -->
    <main>
      <header class="pk-hero">
        <div class="pk-wrap pk-hero__grid">
          <div class="pk-hero__text">
            <p class="pk-kicker">Oct 2026 · A class book and its website · Python, InDesign scripts, Astro</p>
            <h1 class="pk-title">A class book, <em>by script</em></h1>
            <p class="pk-sub">Thirty-one students, three islands: eight weeks of groundwork, ten days of making</p>
            <p class="pk-standfirst">
              Our Design VI studio read three islands of the Greater Malé Region, Malé, Villimalé and
              Hulhumalé, and redrew each as a masterplan. The research, the drawings, the writing and the
              masterplans are the class's own, weeks of work by thirty-one students. This page is about
              the frame they sit in. I built the tooling that set the book, with Claude Code in the
              desktop app, and it was an absolute blast: the most fun I have had coding since AI took
              the fun out of it, and this one put it back. Python drew the page templates, scripts drove InDesign, and because
              everything ended up so neatly organised, the finished book became a website in about a
              day.
            </p>
            <dl class="pk-facts">
              <div><dt>Made</dt><dd>Eight weeks of groundwork, then 4 to 13 October 2026</dd></div>
              <div><dt>Written in</dt><dd>Python, InDesign's JavaScript, Astro</dd></div>
              <div><dt>The book</dt><dd>198 pages, three chapters</dd></div>
              <div><dt>Status</dt><dd>Draft submitted; website live</dd></div>
            </dl>
            <div class="pk-actions">
              <a class="btn btn--light" href="https://seven-leven.github.io/arc307-book/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
                Read the web edition
                <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M7 17L17 7M8 7h9v9"/></svg>
              </a>
              <a class="link-arrow link-arrow--quiet" href="https://github.com/seven-leven/arc307-book" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Source on GitHub</a>
            </div>
          </div>
          <figure class="pk-hero__media">
            <img
              src="assets/projects/class-book/site-home.webp"
              alt="The web edition's home page: the title Greater Malé Region, a short introduction to the three islands, the cover's three triangles in red, green and blue, and a grid of chapters by island"
              width="2000" height="1250" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"
            >
            <figcaption>The web edition, built from the finished book</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>
      </header>

      <nav class="pk-subnav" aria-label="On this page" data-spy>
        <div class="pk-wrap pk-subnav__row">
          <a href="#itch">The itch</a>
          <a href="#templates">Templates first</a>
          <a href="#indesign">InDesign by script</a>
          <a href="#story">One story</a>
          <a href="#web">A website in a day</a>
          <a href="#people">The people</a>
          <a href="#learned">What I learned</a>
        </div>
      </nav>

      <div class="pk-wrap pk-body">
        <section id="itch" class="animate-on-scroll-target">
          <h2>The itch</h2>
          <p>
            A class book is thirty-one people's work in one binding. Three groups, one per island, each
            drawing in its own software and its own style, with ten or eleven students in each. The book
            had to read as one: the same headers, the same type, the same numbering for every figure, and
            the three islands always in the same order, so a reader could set Malé's chapter against
            Hulhumalé's and compare like with like.
          </p>
          <p>
            Doing that by hand means checking two hundred pages for the same dozen rules, again after every
            change. I had a feeling a lot of it could be written down once and done by code.
          </p>
          <p>
            The groundwork started on the first day of the semester. As editor-in-chief I treated the book as
            a project of its own, and so did the class: an editorial team with a member from each group, a
            leader for each group and a lead for each chapter, and a Gantt chart more than a few of us grew
            tired of seeing. We agreed early on a shared template, typeface and colour system, and one way of
            presenting the numbers, so a land-use table from Malé could sit next to one from Hulhumalé and be
            read the same way.
          </p>
          <p>
            That was eight weeks of groundwork before ten days of making. On this book the organising was not
            a detour from the design work; it was the design work. Everything below is only possible because
            of it.
          </p>
          <figure>
            <img
              src="assets/projects/class-book/cover.webp"
              alt="The book's wrap-around cover: on the front, Greater Malé Region above three triangles drawn in red, green and blue line patterns, one per island, inside faint concentric arcs; on the back, Three islands, one region"
              width="2000" height="967" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
            >
            <figcaption>The wrap-around cover: one triangle per island, in its own colour</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </section>

        <section id="templates" class="animate-on-scroll-target">
          <h2>Templates first</h2>
          <p>
            The first thing I used Claude Code for was a standard template. A Python script draws each page
            of the book as an SVG, with the proper header and footer already on it: the section label on the
            left, the three island names on the right with this page's island in full colour, the page number
            on the outer edge. The groups opened their pages in Illustrator and pasted their drawings in.
          </p>
          <p>
            The whole look lives in one file. Every font is Century Gothic, in eight text styles and five
            sizes, and each island has its colour: Malé red, Villimalé green, Hulhumalé blue. Change that file
            and every page changes with it.
          </p>
          <p>
            Before, the only things decided for the whole book were the font, the sizes and the colours.
            Everything else was up to each student, and whoever compiled the book had to clean every page up
            by hand. This time a design guide came first and decided the rest as well:
          </p>
          <ul class="pk-list">
            <li>where the header and footer go, and what each carries on a left page and a right one;</li>
            <li>the edge bars that mark the chapter, and the island names that show whose page it is;</li>
            <li>how every figure and table is numbered: section, running number, island letter, as in <code>Figure 1.6.4M</code>;</li>
            <li>the order of the three islands, always Malé, Villimalé, Hulhumalé;</li>
            <li>and what to leave out: rules in tables, grey placeholder bars, boxed photo slots.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            That changed when a decision had to be made. Before, something like the margins had to be fixed
            weeks in advance, because changing it later meant going through every page by hand. Now the
            margins, the sizes and the spacing could all be adjusted in one prompt, late, once we knew what
            the pages actually needed.
          </p>
          <p>
            The table of contents is generated the same way, and so are the
            design guides, the chapter openings and the closing spreads that set the three islands side by
            side.
          </p>
          <div class="pk-grid pk-grid--2">
            <figure>
              <img
                src="assets/projects/class-book/styles.webp"
                alt="The style guide spread: eight type styles from page title to small caps with their sizes, and a diagram of the header and footer with the section label, island indicator, edge bars, page number and university line"
                width="2000" height="1000" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
              >
              <figcaption>The design guide: eight styles, five sizes, one font, and where the header and footer go</figcaption>
            </figure>
            <figure>
              <img
                src="assets/projects/class-book/contents.webp"
                alt="The table of contents spread: chapters 01 to 03 with every section's page number given three times, in red, green and blue, one per island"
                width="2000" height="1000" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
              >
              <figcaption>The contents: a coloured number is that island's page</figcaption>
            </figure>
          </div>
          <dl class="pk-stats">
            <div><dt>62</dt><dd>Python scripts that draw pages</dd></div>
            <div><dt>122</dt><dd>page templates, each with a preview</dd></div>
            <div><dt>1</dt><dd>file holding the whole look</dd></div>
          </dl>
        </section>

        <section id="indesign" class="animate-on-scroll-target">
          <h2>InDesign by script</h2>
          <p>
            Then I found out Claude could write InDesign scripts. InDesign runs JavaScript, and it can be
            started and driven from PowerShell, so a script can open the book, place a chapter, set every
            caption and save, while I watch.
          </p>
          <p>
            We set it up like code. The real book was the main file, and only checked chapters went into it.
            Each group's file was copied onto a branch, cleaned and rebuilt there, then merged. Before every
            scripted change the main file was backed up, and the backup's name says what came next:
            "before Chapter 02 merge", "before contents fix".
          </p>
          <p>
            The final merge and format ran for two days back to back, on 8 and 9 October, on two accounts so
            the work never had to stop. Scripts set the paragraph styles on every piece of text in InDesign
            and in Illustrator, numbered every figure, built the lists of figures and tables from the labels,
            and checked the book against its own rules:
          </p>
          <pre class="pk-code"><b>// for every text frame on every page</b>
if (!BOOK_STYLES[style])          report("a style outside the guide");
if (font != "Century Gothic")     report("the wrong font");
if (!SIZES[size])                 report("a size off the scale");
<b>// reads the book; changes nothing</b></pre>
          <p class="pk-caption">From the style check, shortened.</p>
          <h3>The parts no sane person does by hand</h3>
          <p>
            Scripts also made the parts of a book nobody wants to make. The lists of figures and tables, four
            pages, are built from the labels on the pages themselves, so they can't disagree with them. The
            glossary takes two pages. And Appendix A sets out the data behind the class's toolkits as nine
            neatly formatted pages of tables, for the digital edition: exactly the kind of job that never gets
            done when it has to be done by hand.
          </p>

          <h3>A handover, so the work outlives a session</h3>
          <p>
            Two days on two accounts raised a problem: a session runs out of room, and the next one starts
            knowing nothing. Explaining the book again every time would have eaten the work. So I worked out
            a handover. <code>CLAUDE.md</code> holds the rules and the decisions not to reopen, and Claude reads
            it by itself when it opens the folder. <code>HANDOVER.md</code> holds where every part stands now,
            short enough to read in one go, with a table of which file to read for which job. The full history
            of every fix goes in a separate log, not in the handover.
          </p>
          <pre class="pk-code"><b>CLAUDE.md</b>      the rules, read first, every time
<b>HANDOVER.md</b>    where every part stands now
<b>…_LOG.md</b>       how it got there, searched only when needed</pre>
          <p>
            The rule at the bottom of the handover does the rest: when you finish a piece of work, update the
            tables, and put the detail in the log. Any session on either account, or a fresh agent, could pick
            up where the last one stopped. By the end the folder held 48 Markdown files, and none of them was
            code.
          </p>
          <p>
            That is what gave us the freedom I liked most. A group could rework a whole chapter, or I could
            move one, without anyone worrying about everything else: the numbering, the contents and the
            styles would all be put right again by running the scripts.
          </p>
          <dl class="pk-stats">
            <div><dt>33</dt><dd>InDesign scripts and their data</dd></div>
            <div><dt>44</dt><dd>backups of the book, each named for the change after it</dd></div>
            <div><dt>316</dt><dd>linked files in the book, none broken</dd></div>
          </dl>
        </section>

        <section id="story" class="animate-on-scroll-target">
          <h2>One story through the book</h2>
          <p>
            Thirty-one people wrote this book, each group its own pages. In a class book that usually shows:
            each page reads the way its maker wrote it, and nothing runs through them all. We study
            architecture, not literature, and none of us had kept one thought going across two hundred pages
            before.
          </p>
          <p>
            So the thread was written down first. <code>NARRATIVE.md</code> sets out the book's argument, its
            voice, what each chapter and section has to do, and each island's story. One more guide covers
            Chapter 02, and one per island covers its masterplan. Every group wrote to the same guides, and
            every page was checked against them, so the three islands' chapters answer the same questions in
            the same voice.
          </p>
          <p class="pk-quote">
            Public life depends on how a place is designed, more than on how dense or how new it is.
          </p>
          <p class="pk-caption">The book's argument: the one idea the narrative guide says runs through all of it.</p>
          <pre class="pk-code"><b>do</b>                            <b>avoid</b>
"we", the studio              "the researchers"
present tense for the plan    "will be", "aims to"
one idea a sentence           "while", "thereby"
a number with its unit        "many", "significant"
street, shade, homes          permeability, activation</pre>
          <p class="pk-caption">From the guide's rules for the voice, shortened.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="web" class="animate-on-scroll-target">
          <h2>A website in a day</h2>
          <p>
            On 10 October the draft went in. Since everything was there and neatly organised, the text in
            its styles and the figures named and numbered, turning the book into a website took about a day.
            Scripts read the words and tables out of the finished book, and the site is built with Astro from
            the same colours and type, sized for a screen. The printed draft carries a QR code to it.
          </p>
          <p>
            The design guide came first, as always, then the site, in twelve pull requests merged on 13
            October. You pick an island and stay on it; the island names at the top keep you on the same
            section and change the island, and Compare shows any section for all three side by side.
          </p>
          <figure>
            <img
              src="assets/projects/class-book/web-guide.webp"
              alt="The web edition's design guide board: the island colour ramps, the book's type styles sized for screens, the home page as a grid of chapters and islands, a section page with its rail, a phone layout, compare mode, components and a build order"
              width="2000" height="1467" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
            >
            <figcaption>The web design guide, drawn before anything was built</figcaption>
          </figure>
          <figure>
            <img
              src="assets/projects/class-book/site-masterplan.webp"
              alt="Malé's masterplan chapter on the website: a section rail from 3.0 to 3.10 on the left, and the vision, Rebalancing Malé towards urban life, with a diagram moving from back of house to front of house"
              width="2000" height="1250" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
            >
            <figcaption>Malé's masterplan as a real web page, not a picture of the book page</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </section>

        <section id="people" class="animate-on-scroll-target">
          <h2>The people who made it</h2>
          <p>
            Everything above is the frame. The book is what went in it, and that was the class's.
          </p>
          <ul class="pk-list">
            <li><strong>The three groups</strong> read their texts, built the field instruments, walked their islands, counted people on site, drew every map and wrote every page.</li>
            <li><strong>The group leaders</strong> held their groups to the guides for eight weeks, when nobody likes being told where their header goes.</li>
            <li><strong>The chapter leads and the editorial team</strong> kept three groups talking to each other, and checked every chapter before it went into the book.</li>
            <li><strong>Everyone</strong> who redrew work to the templates, sometimes more than once, reformatted someone else's table for the fourth time, and took late changes because they trusted the system to put the numbering right.</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="pk-quote">
            The tooling made the book consistent. It could not make it good. Thirty-one people did that, and a
            frame is only worth building around work like theirs.
          </p>
          <p>
            Neither half would have been enough. The tooling could not have made a book of this calibre without
            the class, and the class's work would not have read as one book without the frame.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section id="learned" class="animate-on-scroll-target">
          <h2>What I learned</h2>
          <p>
            Watching it come together was euphoric. Seeds planted on the first day of the semester, the team,
            the template, the guides, bore fruit in those ten days, and the book that came out reads as one.
          </p>
          <p>
            Organisation is what makes the fast part fast. The website took a day because the ten days
            before it had kept everything in one place, named the same way and styled by rule. Done the other
            way round, it would have taken longer than the book.
          </p>
          <p>
            And a design tool is a program too. InDesign and Illustrator felt like apps I clicked through until
            I found they could be scripted. Once they could, the same habits I use in code worked on a book:
            branches, backups before every change, one file for the look, and checks that read everything and
            change nothing.
          </p>
          <div class="pk-actions">
            <a class="btn btn--light" href="https://seven-leven.github.io/arc307-book/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
              Read the web edition
              <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M7 17L17 7M8 7h9v9"/></svg>
            </a>
            <a class="link-arrow link-arrow--quiet" href="https://github.com/seven-leven/arc307-book" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Source on GitHub</a>
            <a class="link-arrow link-arrow--quiet" href="index.html#coding">All coding projects</a>
          </div>
        </section>
      </div>
    </main>

    <Footer />

    <script src="assets/js/main.js" is:inline></script>
</BaseLayout>
